{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of light that only happens at 5:47 in the morning. It's not quite night, not quite day. The sky is a bruised blue, and the streetlights are still on but looking embarrassed about it. Most people are asleep, which means the air belongs to the few of us who are awake. I like that hour because nothing is asking anything of me yet.\n\nI make coffee in a chipped yellow mug. It has a hairline crack that I've been monitoring for two years. It hasn't grown. I think it's holding on out of spite. I sit by the window and watch the paperboy - who is actually a middle-aged woman in a Honda - throw the news into driveways with an athletic precision I admire.\n\nThis is when my brain is most honest. Before the emails and the headlines and the low-level panic of the day set in, thoughts arrive unedited. Yesterday I was thinking about how we treat memories like photographs, fixed and filed, when they're really more like stories we re-tell ourselves, changing a word each time until the ending is different.\n\nI don't know why we are so afraid of being bored. Boredom is where the good stuff ferments. You have to sit with the nothing for a while before something bubbles up. Most people never wait long enough.\n\nMy coffee is cold now. The sky decided. It's day. Time to reheat it and start over, same mug, same crack, same light tomorrow.",
